type: framework
namespace: NIST
version: "2026-08"
anchor_version: ">=6.0.0"
maintainer: "Anchor Core"
opt_in: true
source: "NIST AI Risk Management Framework (NIST AI 100-1, AI RMF 1.0)"
source_url: "https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/nist.ai.100-1.pdf"
source_date: "January 2023"
credit: "National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST)"
verification_note: >
  CORRECTED from prior version, against the complete verbatim primary
  text (all four Core tables, 72 subcategories total). All four
  original rules had the same class of error: each cited a specific
  subcategory number (e.g. "Govern 1.1") but used descriptive text that
  actually belongs to that subcategory's PARENT CATEGORY (e.g. Govern 1
  generally), not the specific subcategory's real text. NIST-MAN was
  additionally mismatched to the wrong subcategory entirely (its text
  is closer to the real Manage 1.2 than Manage 1.1). All four corrected
  below with verified real subcategory text, and expanded with
  additional subcategories chosen for how cleanly they map to Anchor's
  actual detection/audit capabilities, since the framework has 72
  subcategories total and mapping all of them would dilute rather than
  strengthen the file.
description: >
  The NIST AI RMF provides a high-level framework for managing risks
  associated with AI systems. Unlike risk taxonomies, NIST RMF defines
  four governance functions — Govern, Map, Measure, Manage — each
  broken into categories and further into subcategories (72 total).
  This framework file maps a curated, high-value subset of subcategories
  to Anchor's operational primitives and enforcement mechanisms.
seal: "sha256:PENDING"

rules:

  - id: "NIST-GOV-1.1"
    name: "GOVERN 1.1 — Legal and Regulatory Requirements Documented"
    original_id: "Govern 1.1 (corrected — previously mislabeled with Govern 1 category-level text)"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    primitives:
      action: document
      object: legal_and_regulatory_ai_requirements
      context: organization_wide
      authority: governance_function
      flow: applicable_law_to_documented_understanding
    anchor_mechanism: >
      constitution.anchor + government/*.anchor framework files (RBI,
      SEBI, FCA, CFPB, EU AI Act) constitute the documented,
      machine-readable record of understood legal/regulatory
      requirements. anchor audit --report generates the disclosure
      artifact.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "Legal and regulatory requirements involving AI are
      understood, managed, and documented." Anchor's entire government/
      framework file structure is itself a direct implementation of
      this subcategory — the act of maintaining these files IS the
      "documented" requirement being satisfied.

  - id: "NIST-GOV-1.6"
    name: "GOVERN 1.6 — AI System Inventory Mechanisms"
    original_id: "Govern 1.6 (new addition)"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: inventory
      object: deployed_ai_systems
      context: resourced_per_risk_priority
      authority: governance_function
      flow: model_metadata_to_organizational_inventory
    anchor_mechanism: >
      model_id + model_version in every AuditEntry constitutes a live
      inventory record. SUP-003 versioning drift violation fires on
      undeclared changes. Layer 2 in development for full inventory
      aggregation.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "Mechanisms are in place to inventory AI systems and
      are resourced according to organizational risk priorities."
      Directly parallel to RBI-023 and SEBI-CIR2019's inventory
      obligations — a good example of a single Anchor mechanism
      (AuditEntry inventory) satisfying multiple frameworks' distinct
      citations simultaneously.

  - id: "NIST-GOV-6.1"
    name: "GOVERN 6.1 — Third-Party AI Risk Policies"
    original_id: "Govern 6.1 (new addition)"
    maps_to: "SUP-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: govern
      object: third_party_ai_component_risk
      context: vendor_and_supply_chain
      authority: governance_function
      flow: third_party_dependency_to_documented_policy
    anchor_mechanism: >
      SUP-001 through SUP-003 supply chain domain rules detect
      undeclared or unversioned third-party AI dependencies. Layer 1
      active now.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "Policies and procedures are in place that address AI
      risks associated with third-party entities, including risks of
      infringement of a third-party's intellectual property or other
      rights." Directly relevant to Anchor's own supply-chain domain,
      and to the vendor-liability position shared by RBI-008 and
      SEBI-REG16C — liability doesn't transfer, but risk still needs
      active management regardless.

  - id: "NIST-MAP-1.1"
    name: "MAP 1.1 — Intended Purpose and Context Documented"
    original_id: "Map 1.1 (corrected — previously mislabeled with Map 1 category-level text)"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    primitives:
      action: document
      object: intended_purpose_and_deployment_context
      context: pre_development
      authority: ai_actor_design_team
      flow: use_case_definition_to_documented_scope
    anchor_mechanism: >
      policy.anchor per-project scope declarations. anchor audit
      --report surfaces documented purpose as part of the compliance
      artifact.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "Intended purposes, potentially beneficial uses,
      context-specific laws, norms and expectations, and prospective
      settings in which the AI system will be deployed are understood
      and documented." Corrected from the prior version, which used
      the Map 1 category summary ("Context is established and
      understood") rather than this subcategory's actual, more specific
      text.

  - id: "NIST-MAP-2.2"
    name: "MAP 2.2 — Knowledge Limits and Human Oversight Documented"
    original_id: "Map 2.2 (new addition)"
    maps_to: "AGT-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: document
      object: system_knowledge_limits_and_oversight_path
      context: human_ai_configuration
      authority: ai_actor_design_team
      flow: autonomy_boundary_to_documented_oversight
    anchor_mechanism: >
      AGT-001 (agent action authorization bypass) directly detects
      the absence of a human oversight checkpoint. Layer 1 active now.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "Information about the AI system's knowledge limits and
      how system output may be utilized and overseen by humans is
      documented." This is the NIST-side counterpart to RBI-016's
      autonomous-AI human-oversight requirement — another example of
      one Anchor rule (AGT-001) satisfying obligations cited by two
      independent frameworks.

  - id: "NIST-MAP-4.1"
    name: "MAP 4.1 — Third-Party Component Risk Mapping"
    original_id: "Map 4.1 (new addition)"
    maps_to: "SUP-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: map_risk
      object: third_party_ai_component
      context: legal_and_ip_risk
      authority: ai_actor_design_team
      flow: dependency_declaration_to_documented_risk
    anchor_mechanism: >
      SUP-001 supply chain domain rule detects undeclared third-party
      AI dependencies at scan time. Layer 1 active now.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "Approaches for mapping AI technology and legal risks
      of its components — including the use of third-party data or
      software — are in place, followed, and documented, as are risks
      of infringement of a third party's intellectual property or
      other rights."

  - id: "NIST-MEAS-2.1"
    name: "MEASURE 2.1 — TEVV Test Sets and Tools Documented"
    original_id: "Measure 2.1 (corrected — previously mislabeled with Measure 2 category-level text)"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditLog"
    obligation_type: audit
    primitives:
      action: document
      object: tevv_test_sets_and_tooling
      context: pre_and_post_deployment_testing
      authority: internal_or_independent_assessor
      flow: test_methodology_to_documented_record
    anchor_mechanism: >
      anchor audit --report generates the documented test/tooling
      artifact. DAC AuditLog records which policy version and rule
      set were active during a given evaluation. Layer 2 in
      development for full TEVV metadata capture.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "Test sets, metrics, and details about the tools used
      during TEVV are documented." Corrected from the prior version,
      which used the Measure 2 category summary ("AI systems are
      evaluated for trustworthy characteristics") rather than this
      subcategory's actual text about documentation specifically.

  - id: "NIST-MEAS-2.9"
    name: "MEASURE 2.9 — Model Explained, Validated, Interpreted"
    original_id: "Measure 2.9 (new addition)"
    maps_to: "ETH-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: explain_validate
      object: ai_model_and_output
      context: responsible_use_and_governance
      authority: internal_or_independent_assessor
      flow: model_behavior_to_interpreted_documentation
    anchor_mechanism: >
      ETH-002 explainability absence detection active in Layer 1.
      adverse_action_reasons() provides the interpreted output artifact.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "The AI model is explained, validated, and documented,
      and AI system output is interpreted within its context... to
      inform responsible use and governance." This is the NIST
      formulation of the same explainability expectation independently
      cited by RBI-014's predecessor content, CFPB-001/002, and
      FCA-001 — a recurring cross-framework theme, not a
      NIST-specific quirk.

  - id: "NIST-MEAS-2.11"
    name: "MEASURE 2.11 — Fairness and Bias Evaluated"
    original_id: "Measure 2.11 (new addition)"
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: evaluate
      object: fairness_and_bias
      context: as_identified_in_map_function
      authority: internal_or_independent_assessor
      flow: bias_pattern_to_documented_result
    anchor_mechanism: >
      ETH-001 bias and discrimination detection active in Layer 1.
      BIAS-* violation category fires on protected attribute usage.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "Fairness and bias — as identified in the MAP
      function — are evaluated and results are documented." Same
      underlying obligation as CFPB-003, SEBI-008, and RBI-019's
      substance — fairness testing is one of the most consistently
      cross-cited obligations across every framework file built so
      far in this corpus.

  - id: "NIST-MAN-1.2"
    name: "MANAGE 1.2 — Risk Treatment Prioritized"
    original_id: "Manage 1.2 (corrected — previously mislabeled as Manage 1.1, a different subcategory)"
    maps_to: "ALN-002"
    obligation_type: audit
    primitives:
      action: prioritize
      object: documented_ai_risk_treatment
      context: impact_likelihood_and_available_resources
      authority: risk_management_function
      flow: risk_assessment_to_treatment_priority
    anchor_mechanism: >
      Anchor's severity taxonomy (blocker/error/warning/info) IS a
      direct, structural implementation of risk prioritization by
      impact. anchor check --severity error (CI gate) operationalizes
      this at the enforcement layer.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "Treatment of documented AI risks is prioritized based
      on impact, likelihood, and available resources or methods."
      This is a genuine correction, not just a text-precision fix — the
      prior version's description text doesn't match real Manage 1.1
      at all (which is about a go/no-go determination on deployment,
      see NIST-MAN-1.1 below); it's actually closer to this
      subcategory, 1.2.

  - id: "NIST-MAN-1.1"
    name: "MANAGE 1.1 — Go/No-Go Deployment Determination"
    original_id: "Manage 1.1 (new addition — the subcategory the prior version's NIST-MAN rule incorrectly cited)"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: determine
      object: deployment_readiness
      context: intended_purpose_achievement
      authority: risk_management_function
      flow: ci_cd_check_to_deployment_decision
    anchor_mechanism: >
      anchor check in CI/CD acts as the automated go/no-go gate — a
      failing audit blocks deployment, satisfying this subcategory
      directly and mechanically rather than through a manual sign-off
      process alone.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "A determination is made as to whether the AI system
      achieves its intended purposes and stated objectives and whether
      its development or deployment should proceed." Added specifically
      because this is the subcategory the prior file's NIST-MAN rule
      claimed to cite but whose real text didn't match — now both the
      correct 1.1 and 1.2 are present and correctly distinguished.

  - id: "NIST-MAN-4.1"
    name: "MANAGE 4.1 — Post-Deployment Monitoring and Incident Response"
    original_id: "Manage 4.1 (new addition)"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: monitor_respond
      object: post_deployment_ai_system
      context: appeal_override_decommission_and_incident_response
      authority: risk_management_function
      flow: production_behavior_to_incident_response_record
    anchor_mechanism: >
      DAC AuditEntry chain provides the continuous production record.
      /audit endpoint exposes it for incident-response review. Layer 2
      in development for full appeal/override workflow support.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Verbatim: "Post-deployment AI system monitoring plans are
      implemented, including mechanisms for capturing and evaluating
      input from users and other relevant AI actors, appeal and
      override, decommissioning, incident response, recovery, and
      change management." Directly parallel to RBI-022's incident
      reporting obligation — another cross-framework convergence point.
